  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There is little change in the synoptic setup as\nan upper level trough currently in the Tennessee River Valley \nhas stalled across the area and will remain there into midweek \nbefore weakening and eventually getting absorbed by a passing \nnorthern stream trough late Wed into Thurs. This should keep \nthe moisture channel open from the Gulf into at least midweek as\nupper level southerly winds remain in place advecting moisture \nnorthwards. A few shortwaves will transit around the base of \nthe upper trough and lift north through the Carolinas through\nWed. As the upper trough dissipates/gets absorbed later this \nweek, any mid level forcing shifts farther inland away from ENC.\n\nA stagnant surface front to our west will move little before \ndissipating on Wed. Given moist, southerly flow across the\nregion combined with high PWATs (2.3+ inches), any thunderstorms\nthat do form have the potential to produce heavy rainfall. Any \naddition areas where localized flash flooding could occur are \nconfined to areas along the Crystal Coast and along the OBX \nsouth of Oregon Inlet.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...By late week the area returns to climo or below\nclimo PoPs as a lack of mid level shortwaves or significant \nforcing will be noted across Eastern NC. This will likely \nresult in daily chances for showers and thunderstorms along the\nsea-breeze, but once again these chances should be isolated at \nbest given the overall setup. After a cool start to the week \ntemperatures begin to rebound back into the upper 80s to low \n90s inland and mid 80s along the OBX.",
